  "slug": "associated-oyster-3ec6",
  "summary": "Spatial and single-nucleus methods are now being used to derive more reliable readouts from brain and aging tissue data.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that conclusions at cell and spatial resolution depend on upstream choices of representation, normalization, and modeling context. A new normalization for snRNA-seq and multiome data links suppression of neuronal mitochondrial biogenesis to Alzheimer's dementia, while CEP-IP with CMRF on SEA-AD microglia snRNA-seq ties a mitochondrial MT-CO3 module to cognition. ATLAS applies scRNA-seq-derived probe signatures to spatial mapping, profiling 40M cells across mouse brains to compare cell-type distributions across genetic backgrounds. SpaceDX jointly tests subject-level differential expression and localizes spatial patterns via gene-specific attention weighting. A harmonized transcriptome framework finds that roughly 10% of neurodevelopmental disorder genes are brain-preferential, and proteomic aging clocks reveal organ-specific age signals in mouse RNA and spatial transcriptomes. These last two address related but distinct questions. What is new is treating normalization, probe design, and joint spatial testing as prerequisites for reliable readouts rather than downstream details.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
